ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: MCAS Gastrointestinal Predominant - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is diffuse and crampy rather than localizing to the right lower quadrant, and absence of rigid peritonitis or fever.

Infectious Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Rapid onset immediately post-prandial in the setting of chronic similar (though less severe) flares, and absence of sick contacts.

Mesenteric Ischemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Soft abdomen, lack of cardiovascular risk factors, and clear resolution of pain peaks following bowel motions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of agonizing abdominal pain, rapid distension, and diarrhea necessitated immediate physical evaluation to differentiate a functional mast cell crisis from an acute surgical emergency. Once mechanical obstruction and peritonitis were clinically excluded, urgent administration of parenteral anti-spasmodics and anti-emetics was required to halt the massive fluid shifts and gut hypermotility. Delaying intervention would have resulted in severe dehydration and an inevitable emergency department admission for a non-surgical flare.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Hyoscine Butylbromide (Buscopan) 20mg IM to rapidly halt gastrointestinal smooth muscle spasm, alongside Ondansetron 4mg sublingually. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Zinc, and targeted elimination diets. Advised strict clear fluid diet for 24 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: By slowly sipping an oral rehydration solution rather than plain water tomorrow, you will actively pull fluid back into your bloodstream without triggering further bowel spasms, directly resolving your dizziness and rewarding yourself with safe mobility.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to specialized gastrointestinal dietician.
DME/Safety Optional: None required.
Follow-up: GP review in 3 days to initiate oral H1/H2 blockers and consider oral Cromolyn Sodium.

